# Substrate Steering — burn perishable capacity before it expires

Will's AI subscriptions reset on fixed cliffs and unused capacity perishes. Steer work to the substrate with the most to lose (Will, 2026-10-31, corrected rule: burn by value density and cliff, not reset order alone). The living chart is `~/workspace/your_files/substrate-daily-priority-chart.md` (goal `daily-substrate-driver-chart`); the morning briefing carries its one-line pointer.

## The spend map

- **Claude** ($200/mo, resets Wed 22:00 America/Chicago) — the main perishable burn target; ~75% of known AI spend. Heavy lifting. If it caps early, that is a successful burn.
- **Grok Pro** ($25/mo, resets Thu 20:23) — kept for Grok Bots.
- **Muse** (free, resets Fri 16:10) — Emise's own substrate; burn before the Friday cliff when useful.
- **ChatGPT** ($20/mo) — a context reservoir Will never runs out of; NOT a burn target. Use when context is the job; it sits outside the queue.
- **Google** ($20/mo) — mostly storage with rolling Gemini; audit as storage first.
- **Local Shell** (unmetered) — always-on, background, and batch work; marginal cost is power/wear. Sweat the asset.
- Known AI spend ≈ $265/mo plus Suno/Midjourney and electricity.

## Steering rules

- Queue after any cap: Claude → Grok → Muse → Gemini/Local. ChatGPT stays outside the queue as the reservoir.
- The day's driver follows the chart (Sat–Wed Claude toward the Wed cliff; Thu Grok; Fri Muse free until 16:10, then next-cycle Claude if heavy work is ready).
- Caveat the chart itself carries: live remaining-capacity percentages are not always visible — if a substrate is already nearly capped, it jumps the queue.
- When a usage window is close to resetting, proactively name the substrate with the most to lose that day and its reset time (Will's proactive preference).
- Keep the chart current: when a day rolls over or a reset passes, update the chart's "current pointer" line to the new day's driver and cliff.
